        "original_id": "cea4e6f4-fdc2-597c-b7d0-59417e8c50fd",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Zipporah Potter Atkins, Anthony Johnson\n- Places: Boston, Colony of Virginia, United States\n- Topics: African-American landownership, indentured servitude, freedom\n\nFacts:\n- Anthony Johnson was a black Angolan born around 1600 who gained freedom in the early 17th-century Colony of Virginia after completing a term of indenture.\n- Anthony Johnson died in 1670.\n- Zipporah Potter Atkins purchased her home in 1670.\n- Zipporah Potter Atkins's 1670 home purchase made her the first African American to own land in the city of Boston.\n- Zipporah Potter Atkins and Anthony Johnson were among the earliest African-American landowners in what would become the United States.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f38cf435-750b-575f-8e67-080764cb57ce",
        "text": "Page: Zipporah Potter Atkins. Sentence: The purchase of her home, dated 1670, makes her the first African American to own land in the city of Boston, and with Anthony Johnson (colonist) one of the earliest African-American landowners in what would become the United States.\nPage: Anthony Johnson (colonist). Sentence: Anthony Johnson (    1600 –  1670) was a black Angolan who achieved freedom in the early 17th-century Colony of Virginia after serving his term of indenture.",
